The key structural difference: the oracle iterates `(b, i, j)` with `t = i*block+j` and `break` on `t >= seq_len`; the ref iterates `(b, t)` with `i = t/block, j = t%block` and `continue` on `t >= seq_len`. Different decomposition, same result — and the ref naturally handles the truncation as a per-t skip rather than a page break. Good enough as "independent".

Wait — there's a subtle issue. In the oracle, the inner loop is `for (j = 0; j < block; ++j) { t = i*block+j; if (t>=seq_len) break; ... }`. This means once `t >= seq_len` within a page, it stops (the rest of the page is skipped). The ref's `continue` for `t >= seq_len` skips individual tokens across ALL pages. For `t < seq_len`, both compute the same. For `t >= seq_len`, both leave out unwritten. But here's the thing: in the oracle, once `np_total` pages are done, the outer `i` loop ends (it only goes up to `np_total = ceildiv(seq_len, block)`). The ref iterates `t` up to `max_t = max_table_len*block`. For `t >= seq_len` but `t < max_t`, the ref `continue`s (leaves unwritten). For `t` in pages beyond `np_total` (i.e., `i >= np_total`), the oracle never visits (the `i` loop ended), so out stays unwritten; the ref visits but `continue`s on `t >= seq_len`. Same result. 

But wait — what if a page index `pt_b[i]` for `i >= np_total` points to a valid page with tokens `t < seq_len`? No — `t = i*block + j`. For `i >= np_total`, `t >= np_total*block >= seq_len` (since `np_total = ceildiv(seq_len, block)` → `np_total*block >= seq_len`). So `t >= seq_len` always for `i >= np_total`. The ref's `continue` handles it. Good, consistent.

Actually hold on, let me double check `np_total * block >= seq_len`. `np_total = ceildiv(seq_len, block) = (seq_len + block - 1) / block`. So `np_total * block = (seq_len + block - 1) - ((seq_len + block - 1) % block) + ... ` no. `ceildiv(s, b) * b >= s` always (that's the definition of ceiling). So `np_total * block >= seq_len`. Yes. For `i >= np_total`, `t = i*block + j >= np_total*block >= seq_len`. So `t >= seq_len`. Consistent. 

